Absolutely! Here's a clear breakdown of SQL topics categorized into Basic, Intermediate, and Advanced levels — both in general and specifically for Data Engineering (DE) roles.

🧠 General SQL Topics by Level
Level	Topics
Basic SQL
SELECT statements

WHERE clause

ORDER BY, LIMIT

INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE

Simple joins (INNER JOIN)

Basic aggregate functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG)

GROUP BY and HAVING

Aliases (AS)

DISTINCT keyword
|
| Intermediate SQL |

Complex joins (LEFT, RIGHT, FULL OUTER)

Subqueries (nested queries)

CASE statements

Window functions (ROW_NUMBER, RANK, LEAD, LAG)

Common Table Expressions (CTEs)

UNION and INTERSECT

Data types and constraints

Indexing basics
|
| Advanced SQL |

Recursive CTEs

Query optimization techniques

Stored procedures and functions

Triggers

Transactions and ACID properties

Advanced window functions

Materialized views

Partitioning and sharding concepts
|

🧪 SQL Topics for Data Engineering Roles
Level	Topics for Data Engineers
Basic DE SQL
SELECT, WHERE, GROUP BY

Joins (especially INNER and LEFT)

Basic data cleaning with SQL

Understanding schema and ER diagrams

Loading data into staging tables
|
| Intermediate DE SQL |

Writing efficient joins across large datasets

CTEs and subqueries for pipeline logic

Window functions for deduplication and ranking

Data validation with CASE and filters

Building views for analytics

Using SQL in ETL tools (e.g., Apache Airflow, dbt)
|
| Advanced DE SQL |

Query performance tuning (EXPLAIN plans, indexing)

Handling slowly changing dimensions (SCDs)

Recursive queries for hierarchical data

Partitioning strategies for big data

SQL in data warehousing (star/snowflake schemas)

Integration with cloud platforms (BigQuery, Redshift, Snowflake)
|

✅ Summary
Basic SQL is about querying and modifying data.

Intermediate SQL adds logic, structure, and performance awareness.

Advanced SQL dives into optimization, automation, and scalability — essential for data engineers working with big data systems.


